If you have millions of users to migrate from on-prem SQL Server to Azure AD B2C, the approach must be highly optimized for performance, parallel execution, and fault tolerance.


---

Optimized Bulk Migration for Millions of Users

✅ Key Considerations

✔ Batch Processing → Migrate users in batches (e.g., 10,000 at a time)
✔ Parallel Execution → Use Task.WhenAll() to process multiple batches concurrently
✔ Rate Limits Handling → Microsoft Graph API has limits (default: 10,000 API calls per 10 mins)
✔ Resumable & Fault Tolerant → Log progress and retry failures


---

1. Choose the Best Approach

Option 1: Microsoft Graph API (Limited but Works)

Pros: Official API, supports creating users with email/password.

Cons: Rate limited (~10,000 requests per 10 minutes).


Option 2: Use Azure AD B2C Bulk Import via Microsoft Support (Best for Scale)

Microsoft allows bulk user imports via backend tools for large-scale migrations.

Requires support ticket with Microsoft to enable bulk migration.

Recommended for millions of users.


Which one do you prefer? If Microsoft Graph API, follow the steps below.


---

2. Extract Users Efficiently from SQL Server

Use Dapper + Pagination (Instead of Fetching All at Once)

int batchSize = 10000;
int offset = 0;
while (true)
{
    var users = connection.Query<User>(
        "SELECT * FROM Users WHERE IsMigrated = 0 ORDER BY Id OFFSET @Offset ROWS FETCH NEXT @BatchSize ROWS ONLY",
        new { Offset = offset, BatchSize = batchSize }
    ).ToList();

    if (!users.Any()) break; // No more users to migrate

    await MigrateUsersToAzureB2C(users);

    offset += batchSize;
}


---

3. Migrate Users in Parallel with Microsoft Graph API

Use Parallel API Calls for Speed

async Task MigrateUsersToAzureB2C(List<User> users)
{
    var tasks = users.Select(user => CreateUserInAzureB2C(user)).ToArray();
    await Task.WhenAll(tasks);
}


---

4. Handle Microsoft Graph API Rate Limits

Microsoft enforces API limits (10,000 requests per 10 minutes).

Solution: Implement Throttling

using Polly;
var retryPolicy = Policy
    .Handle<HttpRequestException>()
    .OrResult<HttpResponseMessage>(r => r.StatusCode == (HttpStatusCode)429) // Too many requests
    .WaitAndRetryAsync(3, retryAttempt => TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10));

await retryPolicy.ExecuteAsync(() => CreateUserInAzureB2C(user));


---

5. Track Migration Progress & Resume Failed Jobs

Create a "MigrationLog" Table

CREATE TABLE MigrationLog (
    UserId INT PRIMARY KEY,
    Status VARCHAR(50), -- Success, Failed, InProgress
    ErrorMessage TEXT NULL,
    LastAttempt DATETIME
);

Update Migration Status in SQL

connection.Execute("UPDATE MigrationLog SET Status = 'Success' WHERE UserId = @UserId", new { user.UserId });


---

6. Automate & Schedule the Migration

Use Azure Functions or Windows Task Scheduler

For Azure Functions (Best for Cloud Execution):

Deploy the migration logic to Azure Functions with timer trigger.

Schedule it to run every 10 minutes to process the next batch.


public static async Task RunMigration([TimerTrigger("*/10 * * * * *")] TimerInfo myTimer)
{
    await StartMigrationProcess();
}


